[Music]  workplace vaccine mandates are becoming  increasingly common and of course  controversial with canada's employment  minister carla qualtro warning that  unvaccinated workers who lose their jobs  may not be eligible for ei  but is this even permitted under the  rule of law let's bring in employment  lawyer leora samfiro who knows the  answer let's talk about this can workers  who are denied  or rather who are laid off because they  refuse the vaccine can they have access  to employment insurance  so anthony we've heard those comments  from the minister but it is important to  understand that at this point all it is  is comments in order for this type of a  chain to deprive people of ei there  would have to be a legislative change  there would actually have to be amended  amendments to the employment insurance  act and that's not something we've heard  happening so far the way the law  currently works is an employee gets  deprived of ei if they do something that  essentially makes it impossible for the  employer to continue employing them now  with respect to vaccination it is really  right now an employer's choice to let  the employee go it's not something that  the employer must do if there's no  government mandate so the basic  principle would be that an employee  right now should still qualify for ei if  in fact they choose not to be vaccinated  unless there's a government mandate if  that changes it would require  legislative change at this point we have  not heard of that happening  so let's talk about severance then what  happens in that case because i know  we've talked about this before  but are they entitled to it and does you  know getting vaccinated or not does that  impact  so severance is a big issue a lot of  people have already lost their jobs or  been suspended and definitely because  they've chosen not to be vaccinated but  here's where we go back to what i said  earlier about a government landing  whenever there's no government mandate  that means that it's the employer  choosing to let the employee go they  don't have to and in that situation the  laws of severance would say that yes an  employee that chooses not to be  vaccinated should get severance so for  most of these individuals unless the  employer really can show that there was  no choice an employee that loses their  job because of the vaccine or because of  their employer's vaccine mandate is  entitled to their full severance up to  two years pay  so a big warning really to  employers and companies given this  because a lot of companies are trying to  enact this but they're not doing so  legally correct  that's exactly right i don't think that  employers understand and appreciate the  exposure the financial exposure they  have important for those employers to  get some advice as well so we're talking  about the legality of the vaccine  mandate but the sort connects in many  ways to the viability because in terms  of the vaccine mandate we saw it in  health care or you know long-term care  it was attempted in certain health care  settings but we're seeing as well  because of labor shortages a lot of  these places have to bring back alberta  inviting their 1400 health care workers  now niagara health doing the same for  its nearly 200 workers  so you translate that into the overall  workforce that could have a huge impact  i think absolutely employers are  struggling to keep people so if they end  up losing more people because of the  vaccine and also have to pay them uh for  that privilege so to speak that may  actually have the the effect of putting  a lot of businesses under which is  simply bad for everyone  all right so let's talk shift gears for  a moment and kids have returned to  remote learning this week what rights do  working parents have if they're based in  the office but of course needs to take  care of their children  so in ontario we have something called  the infectious disease emergency leave  which means that a parent does have a  right if their kid has no school schools  are closed to stay home with their child  they have a job protected leave of  absence which means the employer cannot  punish them or do anything to them in  that situation even if your child is  older as long as you're under the age of  18 a parent has a right to stay home  with their kids as long as those schools  are closed  uh real quick i want to ask in terms of  you know what employers can do or need  to do for uh for their employees given  all this so under human rights of the  legislation employer does who have to  accommodate family responsibilities  couple of things that come to mind is  allowing employees to work from home  where that is possible that's very  important another thing of course is to  change work hours if needed to allow  employees to still meet those financial  and family obligations so there is that  duty to accommodate of course that  employers can't simply choose to ignore  we appreciate this as always shedding  light on some very important issues that  is our employment lawyer leor semf  you're always a pleasure sir  thank you

